  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses a complex multi-phase optimization system but is slow and may get stuck in local optima. The key bottleneck is the grid-based coordinate descent, which is O(n\u00b2) per candidate evaluation and tends to converge slowly. The new approach replaces grid-based evaluation with a continuous, stochastic method using Gaussian processes for Bayesian optimization. This has three main advantages:\n\n1. It works directly on the continuous problem, avoiding discrete grid dependencies\n2. Bayesian optimization explores promising regions using acquisition functions\n3. The hierarchical integration strategy ensures global exploration while maintaining feasibility at all scales\n\nWe first optimize a continuous approximation for the 1-period function (by exploiting interval scaling). This reduces the dimension while approximating the original problem. Then we translate the solution to integer grid points using rounding and repair. Non-gradient-based methods handle the non-convexity through probabilistic exploration.\n\nMathematical specification\nVariables: \n- Continuous valuation f(x): [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with E_x[f(x)] = 1/2\n- Discrete samples h_i on a grid with \u2211h_i = n/2\n- Principal variables: embedding coefficients for intervals\n\nObjective: minimize max_lag \u222bf(x)(1-f(x+l))dx\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h_i * dx = 1 (actual integral constraint)\n\nWe discretize the integral using:\n  \u222b \u2248 dx * \u2211f(x_k)  \nwith dx=2/n_points\n\nThe core algorithm is a hierarchical approach:\n1. Create a truncated Fourier series basis\n2. Parameterize f(x) as a linear combination of bases\n3. Sample coefficients with Gaussian process-based Bayesian optimization\n4. Transfer to integer grid via rounding/repair\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Parent initialization:\n   - Validate n_points from global input or default 224\n   - Set grid spacing and prefix arrays for correlation\n\n2. Objective function:\n   ```python\n   def objective_continuous(params):\n       f_coeffs = params.head(4)  # Fourier coefficients\n       f(x) = np.sum(f_coeffs * np.cos(k*x), axis=0)  # Fourier series\n       # Compute discrete integral: dx * np.sum(f_coeffs * ...) ... (details)\n   ```\n\n3. Bayesian optimization phase:\n   ```python\n   from bayeso import bo\n   space = [GaussianProcessSpace(-0.5,0.5)] * 10  # 10-dimensional space\n   optimizer = bayes_opt.optimize(space, objective_continuous, max_iter=100)\n   ```\n\n4. Repair and transfer to discrete grid:\n   ```python\n   def repair_to_discrete(coeffs, grid_size=224):\n       f_approx = (coeffs @ np.concatenate([np.cos(karr*xgrid), np.sin(karr*xgrid)]))\n       # Rounding to grid points\n       h_values = np.clip(np.round(f_approx/dx), 0, 1) * dx\n       # Adjust sum to grid requirement\n       while np.abs(sum(h_values) - desired_sum) > 0.01:\n           # Adjust coefficients\n   ```\n\n5. Search schedule:\n   - Stage 1: Bayesian optimization (70% budget)\n   - Stage 2: Local repair/adjustment (20% budget)\n   - Stage 3: Random flips with probability (10% budget)\n\nSearch choices and variations\n- Primary search: Gaussian Process-based Bayesian Optimization\n- Secondary search: Simulated tempering with cooling schedule when stuck\n- Tertiary search: Coordination descent fallback if direct optimization fails\n- Variation parameters: \n   - Tunable basis size: 4-12 Fourier components\n   - Adjust miscalibration probability when repair fails\n   - Smoothness parameter for rounding transfer\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Grid transfer: O(n) using greedy round-robin allocation\n- Phase runtime: \n   - Bayesian phase: 50% budget\n   - Local phase: 30% budget\n   - Backup phase: 20% budget\n- Termination checks: \n   - Time budget - check inheritors\n   - Stability: 5 consecutive worse iterations\n   - Objective ceiling - acceptable deviation from continuous bound\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Check feasibility: 0\u2264h_i\u22641 & sum(h_i)\u2248112\n2. Compute exact C5 using:\n   ```python\n   def compute_c5(h): \n       # Discretized integral calculation\n       corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n       return np.max(corr) * (2.0/len(h))\n   ```\n3. Check against record: C5 < 0.380876\n4. Maintain separate validation for each grid point reduction",
